Option 1 (Best if you have Admin): Repair Chocolatey properly
1) Open PowerShell as Administrator

Start menu → type PowerShell → right-click → Run as administrator

2) Repair by removing the broken Chocolatey folder

Run:

Remove-Item -Recurse -Force C:\ProgramData\chocolatey


This removes the broken install only. (You don’t have choco packages installed anyway if choco.exe is missing.)

3) Reinstall Chocolatey

Run:

Set-ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Scope Process -Force
[System.Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol = [System.Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol -bor 3072
iex ((New-Object System.Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://community.chocolatey.org/install.ps1'))

4) Verify
choco -v

5) Install Git + VirtualBox + Vagrant
choco install git -y
choco install virtualbox -y
choco install vagrant -y


Reboot if prompted (VirtualBox often requires it).

Option 2 (No Admin needed): Install everything manually (skip Chocolatey)

This is often the fastest on locked-down PCs.

Step A — Install Git

Download Git for Windows (64-bit)

Install with defaults

Verify:

git --version

Step B — Install VirtualBox (or Hyper-V)

If you’re on Windows Home → use VirtualBox

If you’re on Windows Pro → Hyper-V is possible, but VirtualBox is simplest

Verify (VirtualBox):

VBoxManage --version

Step C — Install Vagrant

Download Vagrant MSI for Windows (64-bit)

Install

Verify:

vagrant --version

Step D — Test Vagrant
mkdir $HOME\vagrant-test
cd $HOME\vagrant-test
vagrant init hashicorp/bionic64
vagrant up

Which option should you do?

If you can run PowerShell as Administrator → do Option 1

If you cannot (work/school computer, no admin) → do Option 2

Quick check (tell me the result)

Run this:

net session


If it says Access is denied → you’re not admin → Option 2

If it returns session info (or a different output without access denied) → you’re admin → Option 1

If you paste the net session output, I’ll tell you exactly which path to follow next.
